> How would you think to handle save files from versions with different
> features enabled? (The point I worried about in my thoughts; see [*].)
> The easier way would be to assume a standard feature configuration.

Well, the first concern would be being able to transfer files between
systems that have all the features enabled. In a way that would allow
transferring them also to any future version that has all features
enabled.

I don't think there really are too many things to save that would really
depend on the feature sets in the portable format, and most of them could
be ignored if the receiving end doesn't recognize them (they could give
some warning, of course).
So much is just more compiler-dependent than feature-dependent.. like the
struct sizes. Attributes unknown in the receiving end could just be
ignored, and ones unknown in the sending end could have some default
value. 
Monsters and items unknown in the receiving end could just be ignored and
their inventory dropped on the floor. (How many configuration-dependent
monsters or items are there anyway? The mail daemon, who probably can't be
in a bones file anyway? The tourist quest folks, but tourist quest bones
would never be loaded to a game with TOURIST not defined anyway.
Kops? Not very important, I think.. think about what happens when they
would be summoned but are not compiled in: that's how the bones would
probably have been created in the receiving end.
Saddles? Just ignore them if the receiving end was compiled without STEED.
Scrolls of mail? Ignore.)

So, if the import utility doesn't understand something, it can ignore it.
Or if something conditionally-compiled is considered too important to be
ignored, the export utility could provide some alternative for it.
And I don't really think we should be too concerned about moving files to
an older version.. people using those utilities would probably not use an
old version anyway.

